v1.26 changes (vs v1.25):
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  FEATURE — clock-anchored repeating tasks (the drift stop):
     - PROPOSED BY ALEXANDER O. — the fixed-time anchor idea (rather than
       relative-to-last-finished) was his and instrumental in the final design.
     - WHY: the old spawn rule (next = completed_at + interval) made the schedule
       ratchet LATER every cycle, because the gate was derived from when the task
       was FINISHED, not a fixed clock. A daily chore done at 08:00 the first day
       started appearing at 08:15, 08:30, 09:00... over successive days.
     - FIX: optional `repeat.at` (clock anchor, UTC ISO-8601). When present, the
       spawn is the first grid line `at + k*interval >= now`, so a late completion
       CANNOT push the schedule. Future `at` = first appearance (k=0); past `at`
       snaps to the next line >= now. NO `at` -> legacy completed_at + interval
       (fully backward compatible, no migration).
     - UI admin.html: with Repeat ON, "Can start on" doubles as the anchor
       (sent as repeat.at, defaults to now). No new field added.
     - UI index.html: repeat chip shows the anchor, e.g. "↻ 07:00 d".
     - FIX: double repeat icon on the card (the chip template already prints ↻; the
       anchored branch had prepended a second) -> now a single icon.
     - SAFETY: api.php now REJECTS unknown board keys on writes (POST/PATCH/PUT/
       DELETE -> 400) instead of falling back to the default board, so a typo or a
       throwaway test key can't pollute a live board. GET keeps the default
       fallback; writes with no ?board= still target the default.
     - Verified on the dev server (throwaway registered board, then removed): daily
       past/future anchor, hourly, weekly, legacy no-at (+1 day), one-time no-spawn,
       drift-proof trio (08:00/11:30/23:00 -> all 07:00); write guard 400s on
       unknown key while leaving the default board untouched. php -l clean.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  RENAME — task field `startUntil` -> `canStartOnUTC` (shipped in v1.25):
     - Makes the direction of the gate unmistakable: a task CANNOT start before
       this UTC datetime. Same behavior as before, just a clearer name.
     - `api.php` (authoritative layer): the JSON key, the
       `normalize_start_until()` helper -> `normalize_can_start_on_utc()`, the
       POST-create + PATCH-reschedule handlers, the spawn-on-complete copy key,
       the "nothing to update" 400 message, and every comment are renamed.
     - `index.html` (board UI): the Pending "hidden until it arrives" filter now
       reads `t.canStartOnUTC`; comments updated.
     - `admin.html` (admin UI): form control id/name -> `canStartOnUTC`, the JS
       variables `canStartOnUTCEl`/`canStartOnUTCHint`, the visible label
       "Available from" -> "Can start on", and all comments updated.
     - `css/style.css`: the schedule-control comment updated to match.
     - No live migration: NO committed or live JSON carried `startUntil` (verified
       in dev boards/*.json + tasks.json), so the rename is a clean code-only swap.
       Any payload that still sends `startUntil` is now ignored by the server as an
       unknown key, so an old client degrades to "no gate" — no crash, no 500.
     - `php -l` clean; zero residual `startUntil` / `normalize_start_until` /
       "Available from" references remain in the dev code.
     - Staging data migration: staging's LIVE children.json carried 11
       `startUntil` tasks (e.g. "Empty Dishwasher", "iReady Reading"); the key was
       renamed in-place there (11/11, JSON-validated, backup kept) so those gates
       don't silently die. Dev + git data carried no `startUntil`, so no migration
       was needed there — only staging's point-in-time snapshot.
     - Scope: dev + staging (code + children.json data) + GitHub
       (main @ ed05e53, tag v1.25). Production not published.
